DO  -  10.1016/j.jtho.2023.09.194
AB  -  Introduction: For patients (pts) with resectable stage III(N2) non‐small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), the single‐arm SAKK 16/14 trial suggested significant improvements in pathological complete response (pCR), major pathological remission (MPR), and event‐free survival (EFS) with the addition of perioperative durvalumab to standard neoadjuvant chemotherapy (ChT) with cisplatin and docetaxel. The ongoing SAKK 16/18 trial is evaluating whether immune‐modulatory radiotherapy (RT) administered concurrently with neoadjuvant durvalumab can further improve efficacy outcomes. Methods: In this non‐comparative randomized phase II trial, pts with resectable stage IIIA‐B(N2) NSCLC (cT1‐3 or T4 due to size N2 M0) are randomized to three cycles of cisplatin 100mg/m2 and docetaxel 85mg/m2 followed by one dose of durvalumab (1500mg) concomitantly with one of three RT regimens (20x2Gy, 5x5Gy, 3x8Gy) to the primary tumor only. Tumor resection takes place 4‐6 weeks after RT start and is followed by adjuvant durvalumab for 1 year. The primary endpoint is 1‐year EFS. We report on the secondary endpoints of the interim analysis focusing on the impact of neoadjuvant multimodality treatment on surgical outcomes including resectability and perioperative complications. Results: At the cut‐off date of October 8, 2022, 25 pts had reached postoperative day 30, and a total of 31 pts were included in the safety analysis. Median age of pts was 65 years. Adenocarcinoma was the predominant histology (18 pts; 58%). Stage IIIA was evident in 16 (52%) and stage IIIB in 15 pts (48%). 30 pts (97%) had at least one treatment‐related adverse event (TRAE). 88% of all TRAEs were attributable to neoadjuvant ChT (16% G3‐4), 4% each to neoadjuvant durvalumab (1% G3) and RT (1% G4), and 8% to surgery (35% G3‐4). 81% of pts (n=25) underwent surgery. The reasons for non‐resection were death (COVID, n=1), disease progression (n=3), and non‐resectable tumor (n=2). A minimally invasive approach was possible in 44%. Systematic radical lymph node dissection was performed in 88%. R0 resection was achieved in 96% of pts. 44% of the resected patients had at least one AE related to surgery with highest grade 2 in 5 pts (20%), grade 3 in 5 pts (20%) and grade 4 in 1 pt (4%). The 30‐day mortality was 0%. The pCR rate was 28%, and MPR (≤10% viable tumor cells) was recorded in 76% of all resected tumors. Details of surgical outcomes associated with each RT regimen will be presented at the meeting. Conclusions: The pre‐planned interim safety analysis of the SAKK 16/18 trial confirmed that surgery post‐neoadjuvant trimodality treatment was feasible and safe. The trial will continue with a planned total number of 90 patients (30 per RT arm). Keywords: Surgical interim analysis, Stage III(N2), Combination treatment
