AB  -  INTRODUCTION & OBJECTIVES: Patients with muscle invasive bladder cancer (MIBC) undergoing radical cystectomy with lymph‐node dissection are at high risk to develop recurrence or metastasis resulting in significant morbidity and mortality. According to current EAU guidelines, adjuvant chemotherapy (AC) is advised only in clinical trials. However, a recent meta‐analyses supported AC in extravesical or node‐positive disease. AC treatment compliance is low due to its association with significant morbidity and mortality. Up to 50% of the patients are unfit for AC. Phase I/II/III studies with recMAGE‐A3 have been performed or are in progress indicating that the MAGE‐A3 cancer immunotherapeutic was associated with clinical responses in metastatic melanoma and non‐small cell lung carcinoma and had a clinically acceptable safety profile. It may offer an appealing treatment option as adjuvant therapy if the bladder tumour expresses the MAGE‐A3 antigen. MATERIAL & METHODS: In the Magnolia study the effects of intramuscular injections of the MAGE‐A3 cancer immunotherapeutic in BC patients are being investigated. The study aims to screen ∼1300 patients who received no prior neo‐adjuvant chemotherapy and randomise 273 patients with histologically confirmed urothelial BC (Stage pT2‐3 pN0‐2 cM0 or pT4 pN0 cM0) with expression of the MAGE‐A3 antigen and clinically free of residual disease and metastases after cystectomy in 10 European countries. Patients are being randomly assigned to one of two treatment schedules in a 2:1 ratio, two patients randomised to MAGE‐A3 cancer immunotherapeutic versus one patient to placebo. Objectives are to evaluate disease free,‐ and overall survival, the immune response and a gene signature predictive for response to study therapy. RESULTS: Presently, 390 patients gave their consent, 309 tumour tissue samples were screened, of which 52% were positive for MAGEA3, and 64 patients (53 male and 11 female patients) were randomised. Pathological T‐stage was T2, T3 or T4 in 28, 27 and 9 patients, respectively, whereas the pN‐stage was N0, N1 or N2 in 50, 7 and 7 patients, respectively. A total of 23 (36%) patients had pT4 tumours or N+ disease. The randomisation arms were equally distributed with regards to prognostic groups and centres. Fifty‐five patients received in total 335 injections. For the patients currently enrolled, the MAGE‐A3 cancer immunotherapeutic showed a clinically acceptable safety profile with no unexpected safety concerns. CONCLUSIONS: The Magnolia study is designed to evaluate if treatment with the MAGE‐A3 cancer immunotherapeutic can delay or prevent disease relapse in patients with MAGE‐A3 positive MIBC. The randomised patients are representative for the MIBC population after radical cystectomy. All tumours express MAGE‐A3 and 36% of the patients have extravesical or node‐positive disease and hence are at high risk for developing disease relapse.
