AB  -  Background: Curative resection is the standard of care for localized non‐small‐cell lung cancers (NSCLC). Current strategies to reduce the risk of recurrence include pre‐or postoperative chemotherapy and radiation. Immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) have proven efficacy in patients (pts) with stage IV NSCLC and stage III NSCLC following curative chemoradiotherapy. Pilot studies have demonstrated early safety and ef‐cacy of preoperative ICI therapy in pts with resectable NSCLC. ICI may act more efectively when the primary tumor with infltrating immune cells is still present. The aim of this trial in progress is to explore two strategies of short course preoperative ICI treatment, and to develop predictive biomarkers of response by translational research. Methods: NEOpredict‐Lung is an international, multicenter, phase II, randomized, two‐arm trial in pts with resectable (stages IB to IIIA) NSCLC. Primary endpoint is the feasibility of preoperative ICI therapy, which is continuously assessed. Secondary endpoints include the objective response rate, pathological response rate, disease‐free and overall survival, and safety. Eligible pts are randomized between two treatment arms: Arm A: nivolumab 240 mg q2w for 2 courses Arm B: nivolumab 240 mg plus relatlimab 80 mg q2w for 2 courses All pts will undergo standardized curative resection no later than 42 days afer first dose of study therapy. Postoperatively, all pts will receive standard of care including adjuvant chemotherapy and radiotherapy if appropriate. Up to 60 pts will be enrolled at 3 European centers. A comprehensive translational research program dissecting therapy‐induced alterations to gain mechanistic insights and to identify biomarkers predictive of clinical outcome is conducted. This includes genome sequencing, gene expression profling and metabolomic analyses in sequential tumor samples, as well as functional imaging, immunophenotyping and functional analyses of immune cells in tumors and blood. The study is actively enrolling; first patient first visit was on March 4, 2020. Clinical trial information: NCT04205552.
