AB  -  Introduction: In the open‐label, phase 3 CheckMate 816 study (NCT02998528), neoadjuvant NIVO+chemo vs chemo alone demonstrated statistically significant and clinically meaningful improvement in pathologic complete response (pCR) and EFS, without increasing post‐surgical complications, in patients with resectable NSCLC. Here, we report additional surgical outcomes. Methods: Patients with newly diagnosed, stage IB (≥4 cm) to IIIA NSCLC and no known sensitizing EGFR/ALK alterations were randomized 1:1 to 3 cycles of neoadjuvant NIVO 360 mg + chemo Q3W or chemo Q3W, followed by surgery ≤6 weeks post‐treatment. Primary endpoints were pCR and EFS. In these post‐hoc analyses, EFS was evaluated by surgical approach, extent of resection, and completeness of resection. Results: At database lock (20‐October‐2021), median follow‐up was 29.5 months. Of 358 patients randomized to NIVO+chemo (n=179) or chemo (n=179), 149 (83%) and 135 (75%) received definitive surgery. In patients who underwent surgery, EFS improvement was seen with NIVO+chemo vs chemo alone (Table 1), regardless of surgical approach (hazard ratio [HR]: 0.46 [minimally invasive surgery]; 0.67 [thoracotomy]), extent of resection (HR: 0.58 [lobectomy]; not calculated [pneumonectomy]), or completeness of resection (HR: 0.59 [R0 resection]; not calculated [R1 resection]); HR was not calculated in some subgroups owing to limited sample size or number of EFS events. Notably, median EFS with NIVO+chemo was not reached for most subgroups. Two‐year NIVO+chemo EFS rates (95% CI) were: 81% (66‐90) [minimally invasive surgery]; 69% (58‐78) [thoracotomy]; 70% (60‐78) [lobectomy]; 76% (52‐90) [pneumonectomy]; and 72% (62‐79) [R0 resection]. Baseline characteristics for all patients who received surgery as well as updated results from a database lock of 20‐October‐2022 will be presented. [Figure presented] Conclusions: NIVO+chemo did not impede surgical feasibility in CheckMate 816 and EFS benefit was observed in patients receiving NIVO+chemo vs chemo. These results further support neoadjuvant NIVO+chemo as a treatment option for patients with resectable NSCLC. Disclosure: JS: Consulting: AZ, Merck, Roche, BMS, Novartis, Chemocentryx, Amgen, Protalix Biotherapeutics, Xenetic Bio sciences, Regeneron; Grant: AZ, Merck, Roche, CLS Therapeutics, Protalix Biotherapeutics; Clinical Trial Role: BMS, Novartis, Roche, Merck, AZ
