AB  -  Background: In contrast to adjuvant chemotherapy (CT), neoadjuvant CT (NCT) is not currently a standard treatment for NSCLC, and further clinical trials are required to prove its benefits. However, traditional approaches to improving NCT have been largely exhausted. The development of chemoimmunotherapy with recombinant cytokines and assessment of its clinical efficacy in NSCLC treatment are poorly studied. Methods: 47 pts. With st. 2B3B NSCLC were divided into main (n = 25) and control groups (n = 22), comparable by clinical and pathological characteristics: men 22 (88%) vs. 18 (82%), women 3 (12%) vs. 4 (18%); 4050 years 7 (28%) vs. 5 (23%), 51‐60 years 9 (36%) vs. 9 (41%), over 60 years 9(36%) vs. 8 (36%); 2B st.‐2 (8%) vs. 3 (14%), 3A st.15( 66%) vs. 13 (59%), 3B st.8 (32%) vs. 6 (27%). Squamous‐cell carcinoma 14 (56%) vs. 12 (55%), adenocarcinoma in 11 (44%) vs. 10 (45%). Pts. of the control group received a twocycle NCT: cisplatin 75 mg/m2 i.v. on d. 1 and gemcitabine 1000 mg/m2 i.v. on d. 1 and 8; pts. of the main group received additionally i.v. recombinant protein of tumor necrosis factor fused with thymosinalpha (TNFT. Farmaclon. Russia) 50.000 ED/m2 on d. 3, 5 and 7 of the cycle. Direct results of the treatment were assessed according to REGIST 3 weeks after NCIT. Results: By this time, the planned therapy has been completed for 17 (68%) pts. of the main and 14 (64%) of the control groups. A common response was 12 (71%) to NCIT and 7 (50%) to NCT (p = . 06). CR ‐ 3 (18%) vs.1 (7%) (p = .04); PR ‐ 9 (53%) vs. 6 (49%); SD 4 (23%) vs. 5 (36%); PD ‐ 1 (6%) vs. 2 (14%). 13 (83%) pts. of the main and 8 (89%) of the control groups received radical surgery. Conclusions: Phase I of the clinical trial of NCIT plus immune agent TNF‐T showed a tendency to improvement of the overall response to treatment, a significant increase in CR compared to the standard regimen, satisfactory tolerability and feasibility.
