DO  -  10.1111/ajco.13416
AB  -  Background: Preclinical studies indicate that Receptor Activator of NF‐kB Ligand (RANKL) blockade improves anti‐tumour efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB). Clinical trials combining denosumab (anti‐RANKL mAb) with ICB are underway in various cancers although the mechanism of action of such combinations remains incomplete. RANKL and its receptor, RANK, are frequently expressed in NSCLC. Denosumab demonstrated survival advantage over bisphosphonate in metastatic NSCLC (mNSCLC) to bone; a recent retrospective case series suggests correlation between concurrent ICB plus denosumab duration and survival in mNSCLC. Neoadjuvant ICB recently showed encouraging pathologic response rates in resectable NSCLC. Methods: POPCORN (Preoperative PD1 Checkpoint Blockade and RANKL Inhibition in NSCLC) (ACTRN12618001121257) is a phase1b/2 study to determine the mechanism of action, activity, and safety of neoadjuvant nivolumab plus denosumab versus nivolumab alone. Thirty patients with resectable stage 1A (tumour > 2 cm)‐3A NSCLC will be randomised 1:1 to arm 1 (nivolumab, 3 mg/kg ivi) or arm 2 (nivolumab plus denosumab 120 mg sc) days 1 and 15 followed by surgery at day 29. Allowed surgical procedures include lobectomy, pneumonectomy, or anatomical segmentectomy. Thorascopic surgical techniques are permitted. Exclusion criteria include uncontrolled autoimmune disease, steroid contraindications, and unhealed dental disease. The primary endpoint is to define pharmacodynamic correlates of the two arms, including T‐cell‐receptor sequencing, gene‐expression profile changes, and expression of tumour‐immune markers of interest via flow cytometry and multiplexed immunohistochemistry. Peripheral blood and tumour tissue at various timepoints will be collected for translational research. Secondary endpoints include pathologic response rates, toxicity, and R0 resection. Statistical analysis will be primarily descriptive, on the intention‐to‐treat population. Trial progress:POPCORNopened inMay 2019 and isnowopen at four hospitals in Brisbane (Australia). 7/30 patients have been randomised as on 2nd April 2020. The trial is investigator‐sponsored (Metro North Hospital and Health Service) with funding from AMGEN, Inc and Cancer Council Queensland.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02259135/full
