DO  -  10.1159/000526456
AB  -  Background: Neutrophilia has been described as adverse factor for the outcome of patients (pts) with NSCLC. Chemotherapy‐induced neutropenia has been attributed a prognostic value for outcome of pts with solid tumors. The clinical impact of neutrophilia has never been studied in prospective randomized trial or a post‐hoc analysis in the immunotherapy era in the neoadjuvant setting. Patients and Methods: In this post‐hoc study we analyzed data from the trials SAKK 16/00 (n=227) and SAKK 16/14 (n=67). Both studies included pts with resectable NSCLC stage IIIA(N2) (T1‐3 N2 M0, 7th edition of the TNM classification), irrespective of histological subtype, genomic aberrations or PD‐L1 expression status. In both trials pts were treated with 3 cycles of neoadjuvant cisplatin 100 mg/ m2 and docetaxel 85 mg/m2q3w. in the trial SAKK 16/00 pts were randomized 1:1 to additional neoadjuvant radiotherapy with 44 Gy in 22 fractions over 3 weeks or no radiotherapy. SAKK 16/14 was a single‐arm phase II trial in which pts received 2 cycles of neoadjuvant durvalumab 750 mg q2w. Durvalumab was continued after surgery q2w for 12 months. Neutropenia was defined as neutrophil count > 7.0 G/l. Neutropenia during treatment was defined as < 1.0 G/l. Results: In the trial SAKK 16/00 62 pts (27.3%) had neutrophilia at baseline. Median OS was 19.9 months in pts with baseline neutrophilia compared to 42.8 months in pts without neutrophilia (HR 1.4, 95%CI: 0.99‐1.98, p=0.055). During neoadjuvant chemotherapy 107 pts (47.1%) developed neutropenia. These pts had a longer median OS compared to pts without neutropenia (median OS: 42.8 vs. 26.1 months). This difference was not statistically significant (HR 1.01, 95%CI: 0.73‐1.38, p=0.97). In the trial SAKK 16/14 29 pts (43.3%) had neutrophilia at baseline. Median OS was not reached in both groups. However, there was a statistically significant difference in the two groups demonstrating poorer outcome for pts with baseline neutrophilia (HR 2.5, 95%CI: 0.98‐6.36, p=0.046). During neoadjuvant chemotherapy 24 pts (35.8%) developed neutropenia. This was associated with a trend towards longer OS (HR 1.12, 95%CI: 0.44‐2.85, p=0.81). Conclusions: In the post‐hoc analysis of two SAKK trials in patients with stage IIIA(N2) NSCLC baseline neutrophilia > 7 G/l was significantly associated with poorer outcome. Neutrophilia at baseline is an unfavorable prognostic factor and should be taken into account in the therapeutic strategy. Patients developing neutropenia during neoadjuvant chemotherapy had a trend towards longer survival suggesting that maintaining chemotherapy dose intensity remains a relevant prognostic factor in this setting.
