AB  -  Introduction: Despite surgery is underprovided for SCLC, contemporary evidence supports excellent outcome with surgical approach with a 5‐year overall survival (OS) of 50% in early stage disease. The aim of this study was to analyse OS and Disease‐Free Survival (DFS) of surgically treated SCLC. Methods: We retrospectively reviewed the records of SCLC patients who underwent surgical resection in a single centre (January 2000‐January 2019). All patients were preoperatively evaluated by multidisciplinary oncological team and underwent multimodal therapy. TNM VIII edition was used to re‐stage all patients. pT1‐T2N0M0 was defined “early stage”, pT3‐T4 and/or pN1‐N2 was defined “advanced stage”. Survival was estimated by Kaplan‐Meier analysis. Univariate analysis and multivariate Cox proportional hazards model were used for identification of prognostic factors on OS and DFS. P‐value<0.05 was considered significant. Results: A total of 67 patients (M/F:53/14) with a mean age of 68.1 (±7.3) were analysed. Thirty‐eight (57%) patients underwent lobectomy; neoadjuvant and adjuvant treatment were administered in 9(13%) and 53(79%) patients respectively. The mean tumour size was 3.2 cm (±2.3). With a median number of resected lymph nodes of seven, the mean lymph node ratio was 9.9%. Histopathological diagnosis showed: 36 pN0, 12 pN1, 15 pN2 and 4 pNx. With a mean follow up of 49 months, the median overall survival of the entire cohort was 62 months. Fourthy‐three (64%) patients experienced recurrences (27 distant, 10 local, 6 local+distant). The early stage group (33 patients) had a significant better overall survival when compared with the advanced group (34 patients) (p=0.006) (fig 1). In early stage group the 1, 3 and 5‐year OS were 93%, 73%, 66% compared to 70%; 38%, 33% in advanced stage group; moreover, the 1, 3 and 5‐year DFS were 71%, 47%, 39% and 39%, 36%, 28% respectively. The nodal status (pN0 versus pNx + pN1/2), the lymph node ratio (<9.9% versus ≥9.9%) and the administration of adjuvant therapy (yes versus no) were independent prognostic factor of survival at multivariate analysis (p=0.033, p=0.001 and p=0.015 respectively). Conclusion: Our data shows that surgical approach in SCLC results in impressive 5‐year OS, especially in T1‐2N0M0 patients. Surgical resection followed by adjuvant treatment can improve survival in selected SCLC patients: these results might contribute to develop future combined strategies in the era of immunotherapy. Multicentre randomized‐controlled trials are needed before surgery can be suggested as the treatment of choice in SCLC. Keywords: Surgery, Long Term Survival, SCLC
